        "heading": "Exception handling for vendor scorecard",
        "body": "Classify exceptions by whether the work can continue, must pause, or needs an immediate owner decision. The packet should state what the operator did not do and why, so the owner can see the boundary that was preserved. If the same stop trigger appears repeatedly, change the intake rule or the role scope requirement instead of coaching around it. The escalation packet should be short enough to read on a phone and complete enough to decide without a meeting.\n\nWhen stale cost model is involved, use the highest escalation path even if the vendor scorecard request looks routine. The packet should state what the operator did not do and why, so the owner can see the boundary that was preserved. If access is wrong or unexplained, stop the vendor scorecard work before opening the access review system with borrowed credentials. Do not close an exception because the customer stopped replying; record the last contact and the next attempt.\n\nUse separate reason codes for missing quality sample, conflicting source, wrong vendor scorecard, expired approval, unavailable team lead, and a the vendor scorecard outage. An escalation that ends in silence should remain open; silence is not approval. When the source is unavailable, do not substitute a familiar value from an earlier vendor scorecard; stop and record the gap. Do not close an exception because the customer stopped replying; record the last contact and the next attempt."
